      "message": "note: this is the only spot where we actually use the newly adjusted \u0027mtu\u0027.\n\nie. we tune the L3 mtu of the freshly created tun interface to this value,\nthus to at most 1500 now.\n\nThis will obviously affect transmit\n(because it will affect the default ipv4 path/route mtus via the v4- tun).\n\nIt doesn\u0027t actually affect receive, because that is basically governed\nby what gets received on the ipv6 interface, and in order to support lro/hwgro/gro\nthat already has to be able to handle \u003emtu frames.\n\n[ie. it\u0027s already possible we have a 1500 mtu ipv6 interface,\nreceiving aggregated packets, translating them and delivering them to a 1472 ipv4 tun interface]\n\n--\n\nWhat this means is that this change *should* only reduce the maximum size of transmitted packets on networks where we somehow detect the ipv6 mtu as being \u003e1528 likely ethernet networks with jumbo frames enabled, or wifi APs connected to ethernet networks with jumbo frames where the RAs are not generated by the AP.\n\nReducing such a network\u0027s mtu from jumbo to 1528 seems safe and desirable.\n\nIndeed one wonders, whether we shouldn\u0027t have gone a step further and even reduced it to 1500 (ie. 1472).\n\nBut that\u0027s a less clear win.\n\nOne could certainly image a network/nat64 with 1500 ipv4/ipv6 connectivity to the internet.  And then the path from the nat64 to the phone being ipv6-only and 1528 mtu capable.\n\nIndeed at some point in the past I\u0027ve definitely seen our GoogleGuest nat64 infrastructure happily forwarding ipv4 DF set 1500 L3 mtu packets from the internet as native ipv6 frames with 1520 L3 mtu.  These packets would happily reach my desktop *in spite* of everything (including the nic driver) thinking stuff was configured for L3 mtu of 1500.  Often nic driver mtu is rounded up to support 1 or more levels of vlan tags and/or to a cache line, or 256 byte memory area, or something.  ie. 1500 L3 is often treated not as 1514 L3, but rather as 1536 L2 \u003d 3 * 512, which still leaves room (after removing 14 or 18 byte ethernet header and/or 4 byte FCS) for ~1522 bytes.\n\nHowever, I\u0027d still recommend that RAs broadcast the *correct* mtu to the nat64 gateway and avoid sending out RAs with mtu \u003e 1500.",
